# Resilience and chaos testing

Both SDKs ship a chaos harness that drives your configured [runtime policy](/docs/release-gates) through a weighted mix of failure conditions — timeouts, rate limits, server errors, and cost overruns — against real HTTP calls. Use it to verify fallback chains, retry backoff, and cost ceilings actually behave the way your policy declares before an incident forces the question.

## Run it from the dashboard

The **Resilience** tab under a project's Improve section (`/dashboard/<projectId>/resilience`) runs the same fallback-chain, retry, and cost-ceiling logic as a policy simulation against your project's actual runtime policy — no real provider calls, no API keys, no cost. Configure iteration count and failure mix, run the test, and see per-failure-mode success rate, average latency, and fail-open counts without leaving the browser. Use this for a quick policy sanity check; use the CLI harness below when you need to exercise real provider latency and network conditions.

## What it tests

Each iteration picks a failure mode at random from a configurable mix and drives it through `orchestrate()`:

- **`success`** — a real call to the configured `baseUrl`, exercising the happy path and abort wiring.
- **`timeout`** — sleeps past `latencyBudgetMs` to confirm the client aborts and fails over.
- **`429`** — simulates a rate limit response to confirm retry/backoff engages.
- **`500`** — simulates a server error to confirm fallback-chain traversal.
- **`cost_overrun`** — returns a response that trips `maxCostPerRun`/`maxCostPerDay` to confirm the cost ceiling actually blocks.

The harness reports success rate, average latency, and fail-open count per failure mode, so you can confirm `enforce` mode fails closed (or intentionally fails open) exactly where you expect.

## Run it (TypeScript)

```bash
npx tsx packages/ts-sdk/harness/chaos.ts --iterations 20 --run-id chaos-smoke
```

Flags: `--iterations`, `--latency-budget`, `--max-cost-run`, `--max-cost-day`, `--base-url`, `--api-key`, `--run-id`. Defaults target OpenAI with a `gpt-4o` → `claude-sonnet-4` → `gpt-4o-mini` fallback chain and a 40/15/15/15/15 success/timeout/429/500/cost-overrun mix.

## Run it (Python)

```bash
python packages/python-sdk/harness/chaos.py --iterations 20 --run-id chaos-smoke
```

The Python harness mirrors the TypeScript one: same failure modes, same weighted selection, same runtime-policy shape.

## When to run this

- Before raising `maxCostPerRun`/`maxCostPerDay` in production, to confirm the ceiling still blocks under load.
- After changing a fallback chain, to confirm the new chain is actually reachable under 429/500 conditions.
- As part of a pre-release check alongside `test:regions` and `test:sdk:ts`/`test:sdk:python`, when the deployment touches orchestration or retry logic.

This is a local/CI harness, not a hosted feature — runs are not persisted to the dashboard. Point `--base-url` at a non-production endpoint; iterations make real HTTP calls and can incur provider cost under the `success` failure mode.
